#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "07d823d0-39f8-5d70-8df4-7fd15783e148" :question/math-academy-id "e-5427" :question/problem "Are the following transformations linear?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\nT\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix} = kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},; T\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} + x_{2} \\\\ y_{1} + y_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = T\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ y_{1}\\end{bmatrix} + T\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_1$. Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}(kx)^{2} \\\\ (kx)(ky)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}k^{2}x^{2} \\\\ k^{2}xy\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= k\\begin{bmatrix}kx^{2} \\\\ kxy\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\ne kT_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_1$ is not a linear transformation.\n\nNow, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_2$.\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n$T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}3kx \\\\ kx + 2ky\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}k(3x) \\\\ k(x + 2y)\\end{bmatrix}; = k\\begin{bmatrix}3x \\\\ x + 2y\\end{bmatrix}; = kT_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.✓$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n$T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} + x_{2} \\\\ y_{1} + y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}3(x_{1} + x_{2}) \\\\ (x_{1} + x_{2}) + 2(y_{1} + y_{2})\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{1} + 3x_{2} \\\\ (x_{1} + 2y_{1}) + (x_{2} + 2y_{2})\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{1} \\\\ x_{1} + 2y_{1}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} + 2y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}; = T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ y_{1}\\end{bmatrix} + T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.✓$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_2$ is a linear transformation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087498 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad1c29f7-c0e7-5bed-944f-a2e33579c7b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/id #uuid "8fe1f455-e739-59cb-95c2-0378dff32b8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5428" :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation and $\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}$ then find $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})\n$$\n\nNoting that $\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}) &= T(6\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) = 6T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -30\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -30\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087499 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "381f7ffe-ae4b-5e45-9fa9-e2a6bd8348b3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "e34b53de-72d8-541f-bd04-16d2cbf1e6b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5429" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$. If $\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}$ then find $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT(kv + w) = T(kv) + T(w) = kT(v) + T(w)\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(4v_{1} + v_{2}) &= 4T(v_{1}) + T(v_{2}) \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} &= 4T(v_{1}) + \\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n4T(v_{1}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n4T(v_{1}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{1}) &= \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{1}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087500 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fbd335c-28cc-5f02-b773-f685e8b15417" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Sum Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "fc3159fb-751c-55d6-b41e-4744b80b0ae9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5430" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$. If $\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ then find $\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2)$." :question/worked-solution "First, since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n17v_{1} + 12v_{2} &= 5v_{1} + 12v_{1} + 12v_{2} \\\\\n&= 5v_{1} + 12(v_{1} + v_{2}) \\\\\n&= 5v_{1} + 12(0) \\\\\n&= 5v_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{T}(5\\mathbf{v}_1)\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(5v_{1}) &= 5T(v_{1}) \\\\\n&= 5\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $\\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8532f2a-0470-5497-9a97-ae414dd1060d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262565} {:answer/id #uuid "64acbc19-30c2-5c17-9f51-838f600343d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262566} {:answer/id #uuid "70355b54-e5e3-58fa-ab81-fb569f95146a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262567} {:answer/id #uuid "c2bcc7a1-19ed-5439-9594-9c60437fac16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262568} {:answer/id #uuid "17d72357-aae9-5874-a8f6-71a87231f0e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8532f2a-0470-5497-9a97-ae414dd1060d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262565} :answer-field/id #uuid "2033c514-e2c7-5fab-8823-3a450d6ae616" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2b3861b-a381-5b7c-bc56-9232209a631c" :question/math-academy-id "q-12008" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 4kx+3ky\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81c82a62-ddc1-55d4-a327-e33f832a42fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262579} {:answer/id #uuid "023228f9-10c2-5966-9c94-62d03a03e90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262580} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfcd828-06b4-5211-a97a-3dfa20875dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262581} {:answer/id #uuid "42a980f6-aae3-5b9f-9317-4532c684a618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262582} {:answer/id #uuid "88e075d6-edf6-53f3-9dd3-0a599c94e17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "023228f9-10c2-5966-9c94-62d03a03e90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262580} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d405b92-6b9d-5e4c-ae79-a33f77221e43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e81b13b4-2381-5cfb-82da-8f1e98b1ff39" :question/math-academy-id "q-170638" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(x+y) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "381f7ffe-ae4b-5e45-9fa9-e2a6bd8348b3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110b5302-b280-5712-853b-8b5b4166b93f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262597} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d5414d-ef5f-5aa0-9fda-33725bd98e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "110b5302-b280-5712-853b-8b5b4166b93f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262597} :answer-field/id #uuid "0abef2cb-ec8c-50f5-a770-6319f0fea591" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b50582a5-bd76-5df5-8f9e-e004f2f22ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b50582a5-bd76-5df5-8f9e-e004f2f22ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262600} :answer-field/id #uuid "787535b9-f3f3-525b-b585-49c914003891" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec326bf4-62bc-5b27-93da-03633f6486f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-317722" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(-6{v}_{1}+5{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-kv+hw)=T(-kv)+T(hw)=-kT(v)+hT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-6{v}_{1}+5{v}_{2}) & =-6T({v}_{1})+5T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}] & =-6T({v}_{1})+5[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =5[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "381f7ffe-ae4b-5e45-9fa9-e2a6bd8348b3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50cdf796-aae2-5db5-bb74-ede83d532257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50cdf796-aae2-5db5-bb74-ede83d532257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262608} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd2ec9c0-2bcf-514f-bab6-05a3be1c52c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8a958a7-1cb7-55a2-a620-1e490cda4534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8a958a7-1cb7-55a2-a620-1e490cda4534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262610} :answer-field/id #uuid "533be6a4-61b2-5380-863e-f14e4b613a56" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e8dd3fb-61a6-56c3-af5f-f21f08cff26f" :question/math-academy-id "q-317817" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =3T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] & =3T({v}_{1})+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fbd335c-28cc-5f02-b773-f685e8b15417" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Sum Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6316fc78-fd06-5292-a6bf-05dfdac423ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6316fc78-fd06-5292-a6bf-05dfdac423ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262620} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdc7968b-078a-5b80-b21b-d9d239382b90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262619} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e2f9d36-6ffb-5fd1-a399-c9b8aa52cd3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e2f9d36-6ffb-5fd1-a399-c9b8aa52cd3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262622} :answer-field/id #uuid "22463d5d-adbc-549c-acd3-355ff79c3355" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262621} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "385eeb66-3a18-50fd-b945-5eef7101f944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "385eeb66-3a18-50fd-b945-5eef7101f944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262624} :answer-field/id #uuid "60ffa0e9-5499-53b9-9a8e-c32f9ae0f85d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b16245ec-61ff-5303-bc5d-4c5cddc638fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-317833" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$, find $\\,T(6{v}_{1}-7{v}_{2}).$\n\n| $\\,T(6{v}_{1}-7{v}_{2})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, since ${v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{v}_{1}-7{v}_{2} & =13{v}_{1}-7{v}_{1}-7{v}_{2} \\\\  & =13{v}_{1}-7({v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) \\\\  & =13{v}_{1}-7(0) \\\\  & =13{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(6{v}_{1}-7{v}_{2})=T(13{v}_{1}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(13{v}_{1}) & =13T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =13[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}52 \\\\ -13 \\\\ 39\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(6{v}_{1}-7{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}52 \\\\ -13 \\\\ 39\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad1c29f7-c0e7-5bed-944f-a2e33579c7b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9748f047-caec-5d27-bbfe-fab439fc719a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262635} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c959bd-b3d1-5e97-8069-ecd792c039c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262636} {:answer/id #uuid "74710f2a-9370-54c1-8e25-8c251ada75c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262637} {:answer/id #uuid "8c06956e-c0b1-570d-9b6d-888d3b86cd7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262638} {:answer/id #uuid "63301b59-c472-5563-9947-977125bf95e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63301b59-c472-5563-9947-977125bf95e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262639} :answer-field/id #uuid "2527124d-1280-5c3f-8d85-046b339dfead" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af3683e8-195c-5b56-8022-1f63793fcbe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-37870" :question/problem "If $\\,T\\,$ is a linear transformation and $\\,T([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}]$, then find $\\,T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\,T\\,$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $\\,T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]=4[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]) & =T(4[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])=4T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
